cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "nf1-swarm"
abstract: >
  An agentic AI swarm that compiles the known history of NF1
  (Neurofibromatosis type 1) biology and clinical outcomes into a
  structured, traversable Neo4j knowledge graph. The pipeline uses
  a multi-agent team (Graude, Dirk, Otto, Archie, Stan, Kim, Rey)
  to read primary literature, extract structured claims, ground
  entities to canonical ontology identifiers, assess publication bias,
  curate claims through PI-level review, and deposit them into the graph.
authors:
  - family-names: Way
    given-names: Gregory
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0503-9348"
    affiliation: "University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus"
license: BSD-3-Clause
repository-code: "https://github.com/gwaybio/nf1-swarm"
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-11-17"
keywords:
  - neurofibromatosis
  - NF1
  - knowledge graph
  - agentic AI
  - biomedical NLP
  - literature mining
  - Neo4j

references:
  # ── Claim extraction ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - type: conference-paper
    title: "Mind Your Steps in Biomedical Named Entity Recognition: First Extract, Tag Afterwards"
    abbreviation: "FETA"
    authors:
      - family-names: Shlyk
        given-names: Darya
      - family-names: Montanelli
        given-names: Stefano
      - family-names: Mesiti
        given-names: Marco
      - family-names: Hunter
        given-names: Lawrence
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1973-7051"
    year: 2026
    collection-title: "Proceedings of the 1st Workshop on Linguistic Analysis for Health (HeaLing 2026)"
    pages: "127-141"
    location:
      name: "Rabat, Morocco"
    publisher:
      name: "Association for Computational Linguistics"
    identifiers:
      - type: doi
        value: "10.18653/v1/2026.healing-1.11"
    notes: >
      Core inspiration for the two-stage claim extraction protocol used by
      Dirk (Stage 1: extract) and Otto (Stage 2: tag/ground).
      Key finding: separating extraction from ontology grounding improves
      accuracy because LLMs hallucinate identifiers when asked to do both steps simultaneously.

  - type: conference-paper
    title: "SciClaims: An End-to-End Generative System for Biomedical Claim Analysis"
    abbreviation: "SciClaims"
    authors:
      - family-names: Ortega
        given-names: Raúl
      - family-names: Gómez-Pérez
        given-names: José Manuel
    year: 2025
    collection-title: "Proceedings of EMNLP 2025 System Demonstrations"
    pages: "141-154"
    location:
      name: "Suzhou, China"
    publisher:
      name: "Association for Computational Linguistics"
    identifiers:
      - type: doi
        value: "10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-demos.11"
    notes: >
      End-to-end LLM-based system for claim extraction, evidence retrieval,
      and verification. SciClaims uses a two-pass extraction with Llama-3 7B
      (generate then refine), followed by Elasticsearch retrieval over 4.7M
      PubMed abstracts, then LLM-based SUPPORT/REFUTE/NEI verdict.
      This pipeline informs the extract → verify → curate structure used here.

  - type: article
    title: "Broad-coverage biomedical relation extraction with SemRep"
    abbreviation: "SemRep"
    authors:
      - family-names: Kilicoglu
        given-names: Halil
    year: 2020
    journal: "BMC Bioinformatics"
    volume: "21"
    issue: "1"
    identifiers:
      - type: doi
        value: "10.1186/s12859-020-3517-7"
    notes: >
      Rule-based subject-PREDICATE-object triple extraction over UMLS concepts.
      Underpins SemMedDB (deprecated December 2024, final update May 2024).
      SemMedDB's content is incorporated into RTX-KG2 alongside 72 other sources.
      SemRep's predicate vocabulary (CAUSES, TREATS, ASSOCIATED_WITH, etc.)
      informs the edge types in this project's schema.

  - type: preprint
    title: "GAPMAP: Mapping Scientific Knowledge Gaps in Biomedical Literature Using Large Language Models"
    abbreviation: "GAPMAP"
    authors:
      - family-names: Salem
        given-names: Nourah M.
      - family-names: White
        given-names: Elizabeth
      - family-names: Bada
        given-names: Michael
      - family-names: Hunter
        given-names: Lawrence
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1973-7051"
    year: 2025
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "arXiv:2510.25055"
    url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.25055"
    notes: >
      Hunter lab work on using LLMs to identify explicit and implicit knowledge
      gaps in biomedical literature. Introduces TABI (Toulmin-Abductive Bucketed
      Inference) for implicit gap inference. Relevant to Graude's task-assignment
      logic and to identifying under-explored areas of NF1 biology.

  # ── Knowledge graph infrastructure ───────────────────────────────────────

  - type: article
    title: "RTX-KG2: a system for building a semantically standardized knowledge graph for translational biomedicine"
    abbreviation: "RTX-KG2"
    authors:
      - family-names: Chamberlain
        given-names: Matthew T.
    year: 2023
    journal: "BMC Bioinformatics"
    identifiers:
      - type: doi
        value: "10.1186/s12859-023-05311-2"
    notes: >
      Integrates 73+ biomedical knowledge sources (SemMedDB, UniProt, DrugBank,
      ChEMBL, OMIM, Orphanet, HPO, Reactome, GO, DisGeNET, GTEx, STRING, and more)
      into a single Biolink-compliant graph queryable via TRAPI.
      Used by Otto for entity grounding and edge-existence checking, and by Stan
      for corroborating source enumeration.
      API: https://kg2cploverdb.transltr.io/query

  - type: article
    title: "SemMedDB: a PubMed-scale repository of biomedical semantic predications"
    abbreviation: "SemMedDB"
    authors:
      - family-names: Kilicoglu
        given-names: Halil
      - family-names: Shin
        given-names: Dongwook
    year: 2012
    journal: "Bioinformatics"
    volume: "28"
    issue: "23"
    pages: "3158-3160"
    identifiers:
      - type: doi
        value: "10.1093/bioinformatics/bts591"
    notes: >
      130M+ semantic predications from 37M PubMed abstracts, generated by SemRep.
      Deprecated by NLM December 2024 (final update May 2024). Now incorporated
      into RTX-KG2. Historical baseline; we query RTX-KG2 rather than SemMedDB directly.

  # ── Publication bias and confidence scoring ───────────────────────────────

  - type: article
    title: "Why Most Published Research Findings Are False"
    abbreviation: "Ioannidis2005"
    authors:
      - family-names: Ioannidis
        given-names: John P. A.
    year: 2005
    journal: "PLOS Medicine"
    volume: "2"
    issue: "8"
    start: "e124"
    identifiers:
      - type: doi
        value: "10.1371/journal.pmed.0020124"
    notes: >
      Foundational argument that most published research findings are false due to
      publication bias, low prior probability, and underpowered studies.
      Motivates our separation of evidence_strength (single paper) from
      confidence_score (aggregate, bias-adjusted).

  - type: article
    title: "Footprint of publication selection bias on meta-analyses in medicine, environmental sciences, psychology, and economics"
    abbreviation: "Bartos2024"
    authors:
      - family-names: Bartoš
        given-names: František
    year: 2024
    journal: "Research Synthesis Methods"
    identifiers:
      - type: doi
        value: "10.1002/jrsm.1703"
    notes: >
      Empirical demonstration that adjusting for publication bias using RoBMA-PSMA
      reduces the fraction of meta-analyses with strong evidence from 20% to 5% in
      medicine. Directly motivates Stan's confidence scoring approach.

  - type: article
    title: "Rethinking publication bias analysis methods in clinical meta-analyses"
    abbreviation: "Langan2026"
    authors:
      - family-names: Langan
        given-names: Dean
    year: 2026
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "PMC13244164"
    notes: >
      Review of publication bias adjustment methods (trim-and-fill, PET-PEESE,
      selection models, Bayesian approaches). Key conclusion: 'the appropriate
      response to suspected publication bias is not statistical correction, but
      transparent acknowledgment of uncertainty and proportionate calibration
      of evidentiary claims.' Guides Stan's reporting conventions.

  # ── Standards and ontologies ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  - type: article
    title: "The Human Phenotype Ontology in 2024: phenotypes around the world"
    abbreviation: "HPO2024"
    authors:
      - family-names: Gargano
        given-names: Michael A.
    year: 2024
    journal: "Nucleic Acids Research"
    volume: "52"
    issue: "D1"
    pages: "D1333-D1346"
    identifiers:
      - type: doi
        value: "10.1093/nar/gkad1005"
    notes: >
      HPO codes used for phenotype nodes (e.g. HP:0000957 café-au-lait spot,
      HP:0009732 plexiform neurofibroma). Grounded by Otto via RTX-KG2.

  # ── Agent memory architecture (Milo) ─────────────────────────────────────

  - type: software
    title: "MemGPT: Towards LLMs as Operating Systems"
    abbreviation: "MemGPT"
    authors:
      - family-names: Packer
        given-names: Charles
      - family-names: Wooders
        given-names: Sarah
      - family-names: Lin
        given-names: Kevin
      - family-names: Fang
        given-names: Vivian
      - family-names: Patil
        given-names: Shishir G.
      - family-names: Stoica
        given-names: Ion
      - family-names: Gonzalez
        given-names: Joseph E.
    year: 2024
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "arXiv:2310.08560"
    url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08560"
    notes: >
      OS-inspired hierarchical memory: main context (RAM) + recall storage (disk) +
      archival storage (cold). Agents autonomously page information between tiers via
      memory management function calls. Core inspiration for Milo's tiered retrieval
      (git log → swarm_memory/episodes/ → GitHub issues) and the principle that
      context windows should be managed, not just grown.

  - type: article
    title: "Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior"
    abbreviation: "GenerativeAgents"
    authors:
      - family-names: Park
        given-names: Joon Sung
      - family-names: O'Brien
        given-names: Joseph C.
      - family-names: Cai
        given-names: Carrie J.
      - family-names: Morris
        given-names: Meredith Ringel
      - family-names: Liang
        given-names: Percy
      - family-names: Bernstein
        given-names: Michael S.
    year: 2023
    journal: "Proceedings of UIST 2023"
    identifiers:
      - type: doi
        value: "10.1145/3586183.3606763"
    notes: >
      Introduced the memory scoring function I = recency + importance + relevance
      used as the foundation of Milo's importance scoring (I = α·R + β·F + γ·S).
      Also introduced memory reflection: higher-level observations synthesized from
      raw episodes — the basis for Milo's dreaming consolidation phases.

  - type: conference-paper
    title: "Dynamic Attentional Context Scoping: Agent-Triggered Focus Sessions for Isolated Per-Agent Steering in Multi-Agent LLM Orchestration"
    abbreviation: "DACS"
    authors:
      - family-names: Patel
        given-names: Nickson
    year: 2026
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "arXiv:2604.07911"
    url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07911"
    notes: >
      Introduces Dynamic Attentional Context Scoping (DACS): orchestrator maintains
      a compact ≤200-token registry entry per agent (REGISTRY mode), switching to
      full context for one agent when it needs steering (FOCUS mode). Shows flat-context
      orchestrator accuracy falls from 60% at N=3 to 21% at N=10 agents.
      Milo implements the DACS registry pattern: swarm_memory/registry.md holds one
      status block per agent, fed to Graude for context-efficient oversight.

  - type: software
    title: "Claude Code Auto Dream — Offline Memory Consolidation"
    abbreviation: "AutoDream"
    authors:
      - family-names: Anthropic
    year: 2026
    url: "https://www.anthropic.com/research/components-of-agent-memory"
    notes: >
      Anthropic's production implementation of sleep-inspired offline memory
      consolidation for Claude Code (deployed March 2026). Four-phase process:
      merge, deduplicate, prune stale entries, rebuild index — documented in
      "The components of agent memory" (Anthropic, 2026).
      Milo's nightly dreaming pass (milo_dream.py) follows this same four-phase
      structure: merge duplicates → score (I = α·R + β·F + γ·S) → flag pruning
      candidates → rebuild registry. Scheduled at 02:00 UTC via nightly-dream.yml.

  - type: article
    title: "Novel Memory Forgetting Techniques for Autonomous AI Agents: Balancing Relevance and Efficiency"
    abbreviation: "AdaptiveForgetting"
    authors:
      - family-names: Anonymous
    year: 2026
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "arXiv:2604.02280"
    url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.02280"
    notes: >
      Adaptive budgeted forgetting framework: scores memory entries by recency,
      frequency, and semantic alignment; enforces constrained selection under a
      fixed budget. Formalizes the importance scoring function used in Milo's
      pruning pass. Key insight: importance-driven forgetting (combining temporal
      decay + access frequency + semantic signals) outperforms naive time-based
      or LRU eviction. NF1-core proximity (Gene/Protein/Pathway nodes,
      evidence_strength >= 4) used as the semantic alignment signal in our implementation.

  - type: software
    title: "Git as RAG — Curated Retrieval as Long-Term Memory for AI Agents"
    abbreviation: "GitAsRAG"
    authors:
      - family-names: Anonymous
    year: 2026
    url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14390"
    notes: >
      Argues for git-bound agent memory: ground truth from commits, freshness
      from rebuild, verification from merge. Demonstrates that git log + diff +
      blame constitute a superior episodic memory substrate vs. vector databases
      for personal/team agent memory — auditable, portable, no embedding model required.
      Milo uses git log as the primary episodic store: `git log --author=<agent>`
      gives each agent's complete work history; diffs give exact claim content.
      swarm_memory/episodes/ is the secondary structured summary layer on top.
